A||||6||Tom88gt||testing the V-6 EGR solenoid||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||10-18-2000||09:28 AM||||Still working on the code 32 problem.  Everyone always suspects the EGR solenoid, I do too, but how does one actually isolate this solenoid and test it?<P>I've searched the arcives endlessly and after putting several pieces of info together, I think I have the answer so please give me your opinions.  Until I obtain a good/new solenoid to test, it's only a theory.  <P>Since it is just a solenoid, you should be able to connect the A terminal to 12V power and the B terminal to ground (these are the two lower connections) and energize the solenoid.  Your should hear and feel a solid click as the solenoid moves.  If you also do a continuity check between A & B you should get a very low ohm reading (close to zero) if the windings are in good condition with no breaks.<P>As for the "Code 32" vaccum switch.  If you apply vaccum to the small "jumper" vacum hose from the small square block on the solenoid, that should close the switch and at that point, you should do a continuity check between the C & D terminals (upper two terminals) and observe a very low ohm reading (close to zero).  With no vaccum applied, infinite ohms should be observed.<P>Finally, if my hypothesis is correct, most any GM EGR solenoid should work on the V-6.  The EGR doesn't care as long as it gets vaccum.  The solenoid doesn't care as long as it gets 12 volts from the ECM to operate.  And the ECM doesn't care as long as the vaccum switch closes, providing continuity back to the ECM.  What is important is finding a solenoid which accepts the Fiero wiring harness (or can be slightly modified to do so) and has the same vaccum ports (but not neccessarily the same locations on the solenoid)<P>Comments please?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||10-18-2000||10:40 AM||||get a 5/16 piece of vacuum tubing run it from the TOP vacuum line on the firewall to the EGR port on top of the EGR. disconnect the battery wait a couple secs.. and reconnect.. the computer is cleared.. start the car and drive on a hwy/long rd at part throttle... if the SVC Eng soon lite DOESN'T come back on it's your egr solenoid... and you have just bypassed it and you don't have to replace it. i drove my 86 SE V6 for 18 months like that.. and GT Bastard is still driving it like that! egr works.. passes emissions.. AND no light!<P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||10-18-2000||05:15 PM||||I have an EGR solenoid from a Pontiac 6000 V6 on my 86SE for 4K miles and no egr light.  I had to turn the cylinder around in the bracket but it appears to work fine and no codes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-18-2000||08:04 PM||||Just picked up a new EGR selinoid from Mr. Goodwrench.  $110. + tax.  Keep it with my extra parts and EGR pipe $89 + tax.  Damn 32 code.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-18-2000||11:23 PM||||You want to check that testing logic above.<P>specifically you have to make sure the thing is 5V or 12V. Allot of things the ECM uses are 5V. I don't have schmatics to tell you for sure what the solenoid uses.<P>If it works like most ECM controlled devices it's activated when the ECM closes a switch to ground. That means the solenoid has voltage on it whenever the key is on. You should be able to unplug it, turn on the key, and get a voltage on one terminal of the harness.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||prof bobo||10-20-2000||01:19 AM||||5v vs 12v?  I'm just guessin' here, but since  I design embedded computer systems stuff for a living I suspect I might not be too far off base.  5v is a 'popular' voltage in digital logic components (TTL, LS, etc).  So the ECM 'drives' 5v probably mostly only to things it wants to measure (MAP, TPS, etc). To drive a device like a solenoid or injector it would be more likely to use 12v to decrease the drive current required (if it takes a watt to drive something, increasing the voltage means you can decrease the current yet maintain the effective power dissipation, which is desireable in terms of component cost, heat management, wire size needed to supply the device, etc).  <P>Of course you can try to test the solenoid on 5v.  If it works, fine, the ECM can drive it with 5v and certainly with 12v.  If it didn't work at 5v try it at 12v.  Now if it works then, yep, it's a 12v part.  If not, then either way it's a bad part, eh?<P>BTW, it's probably likely that applying more than 5v to an input of the ECM is more fatal than shorting an output to ground.  Although if it was engineered right the inputs should be diode clamped to prevent damage from overvoltage and the outputs should be buffered/current limited to prevent damage from shorts to ground or 12v.  Hmm, maybe I'll dig that ECM spare board outta the shop and take a look . . .||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tom88gt||10-23-2000||01:44 PM||||Got my new GM solenoid in.  I checked the continuity...damn near zero.  My two bad solenoids couldn't even get the needle to budge.  As for the voltage, the ECM was sending 12V to the "A" termial on the solenoid.  But with no continuity, the voltage never goes anywhere and the solenoid never energizes.  Dropped it in and no "32" yet.  Still, $115 (total, tax and all) is pretty hard to swallow.  But if the code "32" blues stay away, it's money well spent.  Plus the Pontiac dealer said it has a 12 months warranty (but I hope I don't need it).||||reg||7
